{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-74196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/3a919c1ad631af6dfea304db2bdf0ad36fcbff6162f516e8591d7ce9cef3ecca.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/c06f6a453581797596203059e6a1bfd30afd959f469f0408889c6c947a368809.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${P}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${P}^{′}$ is $r=1.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=1$ because $P$ lies $1$ unit *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $P$ are $(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)"] ["math" "(1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-1)"] ["math" "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},-1)"] ["math" "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},1)"] ["math" "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/947857af9d5746ceef623a5d2cc2bf0ac82e6570d7f288727a7591278fc50452.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $M?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/88aeecb46c1f996a7a2a8947a8b5262faa2991992771a960a1e806c2b8915cac.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${M}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${M}^{′}$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=2$ because $M$ lies $2$ units *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $M$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2)"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-2)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3},2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82c