question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/7c49639200c77c264ecb7b58e1eda4053b3ab0fe378d35caa3d3f8bdc6ad5027.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${K}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${K}^{′}$ is $r=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=2$ because $K$ lies $2$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{4\\pi }{5},2)"] ["math" "(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},-2)"] ["math" "(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)"] ["math" "(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)"] ["math" "(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/b8f187c949db780dc4ef62360c76be4a952e2c299257c6d4e9ee80c7254605d6.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $W?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/d1855e0e8f1b8feed4eb2f5fb9dd2a1abadd0ee92304ae0460f9bd130e376b04.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${W}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${W}^{′}$ is $r=2.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=\\sqrt{3}$ because $W$ lies $\\sqrt{3}$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $W$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{3},2)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6},2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-2).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2\\sqrt{3},2,-2)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{3},-2,-2)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{3},2,-2)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{3},-1,-2)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{3},2,-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2\\sqrt{3},2,-2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-3).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-2,2,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,2,-3)"] ["math" "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2,-3)"] ["math" "(2,-2\\sqrt{2},-3)"] ["math" "(2,2,-3)"] ["math" "(2,\\sqrt{2},-3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-2,2,-3)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,\\sqrt{2},-1)"] ["math" "(1,1,-1)"] ["math" "(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-1)"] ["math" "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},1,-1)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},1,-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,1,-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(5,\\pi ,7).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-5,0,7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-5,0,7)"] ["math" "(0,-5,7)"] ["math" "(0,0,7)"] ["math" "(0,5,7)"] ["math" "(5,0,7)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-5,0,7)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(8,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-5).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-168896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-4\\sqrt{3},-5,-5)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{3},4,-5)"] ["math" "(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{3},-2,-5)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{3},2,-5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-2,2,2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-2,2)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-2,2,2)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-2,2).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-2)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(-2,2)$ lies in the second quadrant, we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{2}{-2}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-168898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},2)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-168898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $C$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-3,4,-1).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ,z)$ of $C?$ Express $\\theta $ in radians rounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-3,4)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-3,4,-1)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-3,4).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $C$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(-3,4)$ lies in the second quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{4}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,295\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -0.927\\,295 \\\\  & =2.214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $C$ are $(5,2.214,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(5,0.785,-1)"] ["math" "(5,2.214,-1)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{10},3.141,-1)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{5},1.571,-1)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{5},3.141,-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(5,2.214,-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $P$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6},-3)."] ["math" "(3\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-3)."] ["math" "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-3)."] ["math" "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},-3)."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(2,-2,5).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(2,-2)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,5)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(2,-2).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(2,-2)$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2}{2}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74297" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74297" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2,-\\frac{7\\pi }{4},5)"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},5)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},5)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},5)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74297" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},5)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,-\\sqrt{3},2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3},2)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-1,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$ \n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the third  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{-1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the reference angle and $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff5c04f90479a2c16970a14cfc0484f4666cb1f3ff24f98306e9a7858ed0c6c1"} {"question" "q-74478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)"] ["math" "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},2)"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2)"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "q-74478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/state.json" "file_sha256" "27e7667e5cf82cca9da3d21c31b8dc189c09aff802363e21be3a1985c6ad632f"} {"question" "e-6219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $N?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/53fada6c8e77c7c6fa2a6579b52af21ea03839e134fc2cb3d9529f6cfc01bd4b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/example-6219.json" "file_sha256" "83ac53fb5c403a12f7b6d74dbfd20e25ea10f60ea788312bf4c0bffc9509db10"} {"question" "e-6219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <         2\\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/d60214a4b32c4462d422372aa90e78944649811ce0b1e277f07fe90663e5be94.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are $\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/example-6219.json" "file_sha256" "83ac53fb5c403a12f7b6d74dbfd20e25ea10f60ea788312bf4c0bffc9509db10"} {"question" "e-6221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $\\left(4, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, -8 \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/example-6221.json" "file_sha256" "34f06ea06335616f6f00d9535869fa4d4373325c64fe94bd60fa2af3ff0489d9"} {"question" "e-6221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt3, 2, -8).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/example-6221.json" "file_sha256" "34f06ea06335616f6f00d9535869fa4d4373325c64fe94bd60fa2af3ff0489d9"} {"question" "e-6220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/example-6220.json" "file_sha256" "a8d321c8d1b3cbba4b2650cf77cef6444876c676d66ef2c49d699e3a60543b99"} {"question" "e-6220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <    2\\pi.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are given as $(x,y,z) = (\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2).$\n\n- From these Cartesian coordinates, we know that $z=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- To find the values of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we need to find the polar coordinates of the point $(x,y) = (\\sqrt 3, -3)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nFirst, we find $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(\\sqrt3,-3)$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle $\\theta_R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}, \\sqrt2 \\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/example-6220.json" "file_sha256" "a8d321c8d1b3cbba4b2650cf77cef6444876c676d66ef2c49d699e3a60543b99"}] "usage" {"e-6219" [] "e-6221" [] "q-74300" [] "q-74297" [] "q-74430" [] "q-74299" [] "e-6220" []} "basis" 1274 "retractions" [[17592186129805 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1981/Source/Images/q-74430.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $F$?"] [17592186129798 :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(4, \\frac{π}{6},-2)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$."] [17592186129799 :question/problem "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(5, π, 7)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$."] [17592186129796 :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(2,-2, 5)$. What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A$?"] [17592186128935 :question/problem "What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $N$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1981/Source/Images/deac7441155c1440a00a786c590e7f03.png)"] [17592186128935 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z)$, where\n\n$$\nr \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1981/Source/Images/5ea6b167ebe524618c905f0a1fe8f041.png)\n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}$.\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right)\n$$"] [17592186128937 :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $\\left(4, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, -8 \\right)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$."] [17592186128937 :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 4\\cos (\\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt3, 2, -8)$."] [17592186128936 :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2)$. What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A$?"] [17592186128936 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z)$, where\n\n$$\nr \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are given as\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2)\n$$\n\n- From these Cartesian coordinates, we know that $z=\\sqrt{2}$.\n- To find the values of $r$ and $\\theta$, we need to find the polar coordinates of the point $(x,y) = (\\sqrt 3, -3)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2} + (-3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{12} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(\\sqrt3,-3)$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we first need to find the reference angle $\\theta_R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan (\\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}, \\sqrt2 \\right)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186128571 17592186128572 17592186128573 17592186128574 17592186128575 17592186128583 17592186128584 17592186128585 17592186128586 17592186128587 17592186128589 17592186128590 17592186128591 17592186128592 17592186128593 17592186128625 17592186128626 17592186128627 17592186128628 17592186128629] "immutable_fields" [[17592186128570 :answer-field/correct] [17592186128570 :answer-field/id] [17592186128570 :answer-field/key] [17592186128570 :answer-field/type] [17592186128582 :answer-field/correct] [17592186128582 :answer-field/id] [17592186128582 :answer-field/key] [17592186128582 :answer-field/type] [17592186128588 :answer-field/correct] [17592186128588 :answer-field/id] [17592186128588 :answer-field/key] [17592186128588 :answer-field/type] [17592186128624 :answer-field/correct] [17592186128624 :answer-field/id] [17592186128624 :answer-field/key] [17592186128624 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
